{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01a06df9597c79138a5d7c815576a1e1","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-critic-stale-selected-window\",\n    \"title\": \"Healthy but slow selected instance is rendered stale by the 3x-interval freshness window\",\n    \"spec\": \"Problem: pageView uses 3*refreshInterval as expiry, but refreshLoop waits the interval AFTER collection completes. With default selected interval 2s, snapshots expire after 6s while a legitimate collection can run up to refreshTimeout=30s. This is a missed-refresh signal calculated from an incomplete cycle budget.\\n\\nScope: derive the missed-refresh budget from the configured wait interval plus the existing total collection deadline, with a small documented scheduling allowance. Use existing timing constants and one authoritative calculation; add no persisted duration measurement, extra ticker, or per-instance configuration. Preserve immediate stale on explicit collection error and unknown before any successful snapshot. A silent/stuck worker must still expire even without an error; do not suppress staleness merely because an attempt started. Preserve visible observation timestamps.\\n\\nProof: deterministic time-controlled tests through pageView/real templates show a prior-success selected instance stays fresh beyond the old 6s threshold while within its legitimate cycle budget, becomes stale at the new expiry boundary, and is stale immediately on explicit error. No-success remains unknown. Cover selected/fleet interval changes without sleeps. Exercise one slow-success collector scenario and show eventual successful refresh, with no overlapping collection. Run existing checks. This job owns the elapsed-time regression proposed by canopy-tester-freshness-elapsed-decay; do not pin the obsolete 3x-interval formula.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": {\n      \"agent\": \"forest-misty-step/canopy\",\n      \"principal\": \"none\",\n      \"until\": \"2026-09-04T23:45:59.304Z\"\n    },\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:39:56.656Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/canopy\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/canopy @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: canopy worktree 1788550542915580458-critic, git c203dc9118ace7575d8aab1162f11edc2e3b601a (running build revision unknown)\\nObserved: view.go:172 passes maxAge = 3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID) to instanceView, and view.go:155-157 classifies an instance Stale when now \\u003e= LastSuccess+maxAge even with state.Err == nil. For the selected instance, refreshInterval is SelectedIntervalSeconds (default 2s, inventory.go:27), so the staleness window is 6s. A successful Collect (collector.go:131-208) runs 4+N subprocesses (version, config show, declaration list, one declaration show per declaration, status), each individually bounded by a 30s timeout (collector.go:17, app.go:16), so a healthy selected instance — especially over SSH — can routinely take longer than 6s per cycle. Result: templates/instance.html:16 and fleet.html:21 render the \\\"stale\\\" badge/state-dot for an instance whose collection is succeeding with no error, contradicting the README boundary that stale marks failed/missed refreshes, not healthy ones.\\nRequired: freshness must not report stale for an instance whose refreshes are succeeding; the missed-refresh window must exceed the worst-case collection time for that instance.\\nProposed spec direction: derive maxAge from the collection bound (e.g. refreshTimeout plus margin, or observed last cycle duration) instead of 3x the poll interval, or measure and store per-instance refresh cycle duration; pin with a test where a selected instance with a slow but successful collector renders fresh, not stale.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:45:59.304Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/canopy\",\n        \"text\": \"took\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:47:32.958Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/canopy\",\n        \"text\": \"Acceptance clarification: use maxAge = refreshTimeout + 2*refreshInterval(instance.ID). This budgets one configured post-completion wait plus the existing total collection deadline, with one additional configured interval as scheduling allowance; add no new numeric constant or configuration. At now \\u003c LastSuccess+maxAge, a prior-success/no-error state remains fresh; at equality or later it is stale. Explicit errors remain immediately stale and no prior success remains unknown. This replaces the earlier vague allowance language; the active Builder owns implementation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_by\": \"none\",\n    \"promoted_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:44:02.78Z\",\n    \"promotions\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:44:02.78Z\",\n        \"by\": \"none\",\n        \"spec\": \"Problem: pageView uses 3*refreshInterval as expiry, but refreshLoop waits the interval AFTER collection completes. With default selected interval 2s, snapshots expire after 6s while a legitimate collection can run up to refreshTimeout=30s. This is a missed-refresh signal calculated from an incomplete cycle budget.\\n\\nScope: derive the missed-refresh budget from the configured wait interval plus the existing total collection deadline, with a small documented scheduling allowance. Use existing timing constants and one authoritative calculation; add no persisted duration measurement, extra ticker, or per-instance configuration. Preserve immediate stale on explicit collection error and unknown before any successful snapshot. A silent/stuck worker must still expire even without an error; do not suppress staleness merely because an attempt started. Preserve visible observation timestamps.\\n\\nProof: deterministic time-controlled tests through pageView/real templates show a prior-success selected instance stays fresh beyond the old 6s threshold while within its legitimate cycle budget, becomes stale at the new expiry boundary, and is stale immediately on explicit error. No-success remains unknown. Cover selected/fleet interval changes without sleeps. Exercise one slow-success collector scenario and show eventual successful refresh, with no overlapping collection. Run existing checks. This job owns the elapsed-time regression proposed by canopy-tester-freshness-elapsed-decay; do not pin the obsolete 3x-interval formula.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:39:32.492Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-04T19:47:32.958Z\",\n    \"derived\": {\n      \"terminal\": false,\n      \"waiting\": false,\n      \"open\": true,\n      \"live\": true,\n      \"takeable\": false\n    }\n  }\n]\nEXIT:0\n====takeable====\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"canopy-tester-envelope-schema-validation\",\n    \"title\": \"CLI envelope schema version mismatch and envelope malformed JSON rejection lack regression cover\",\n    \"spec\": \"Defend the public Collector.Collect protocol boundary without changing forest.cli.v2 policy. Using the existing injectable CommandRunner, return malformed envelopes and assert a command-attributed CLIError rather than an accepted snapshot. Cover unsupported schema, non-object/malformed JSON, trailing JSON, omitted required envelope fields, and null args. Keep the existing command-mismatch and command-failure tests; do not duplicate them. Cases must use otherwise-valid command data so each fails for its intended reason. Prove collection stops at the offending command and valid envelopes still succeed. No source-string assertions, new decoding abstraction, or mock-echo tests. Run focused collector tests and repository checks.\",\n    \"repo\": \"misty-step/canopy\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"lease\": null,\n    \"ask\": null,\n    \"proof\": null,\n    \"abandoned\": false,\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"at\": \"2026-09-04T19:39:32.824Z\",\n        \"by\": \"forest-misty-step/canopy\",\n        \"text\": \"filed-by: forest-misty-step/canopy @ misty-step/canopy\\ndeployment: worktree 1788550542912867580-tester (git c203dc9118ace7575d8aab1162f11edc2e3b601a), running PID 704790 build unknown\\nSurface: collector.go:297 (cliCollector.runJSON), collector.go:343 (decodeCLIEnvelope), collector.go:138 (cliCollector.Collect). Behaviors: Canopy relies strictly on the forest.cli.v2 JSON protocol envelope. When a command runner returns an envelope with an invalid or unexpected schema (e.g. \\\"forest.cli.v1\\\"), missing envelope fields (schema, command, args, exit), or trailing JSON data, Collect must reject the envelope with a *CLIError identifying the schema mismatch or envelope error, and terminate collection without accepting invalid data. Failing example: execute collector.Collect where the runner outputs an envelope with Schema: \\\"forest.cli.v1\\\" or with missing schema field or trailing JSON; today collector_test.go only tests command mismatch (TestCLICollectorRejectsEnvelopeCommandMismatch) and exit status failures; zero tests exercise schema mismatch or envelope JSON structural validation. Acceptance: a Builder adds test cases in collector_test.go asserting that Collect returns a *CLIError with the message mentioning invalid schema when the envelope schema is not \\\"forest.cli.v2\\\", and fails when envelope top-level fields are missing or trailing JSON is present. Observed: collector.go:297-300 and collector.go:356-361 check the schema and top-level envelope fields, but grep reveals no test references invalid schema or envelope field parsing errors. Required: unit tests in collector_test.go covering envelope schema mismatch, missing required envelope keys, and trailing JSON in command output. Proposed test-work: add collector tests in collector_test.go verifying that envelopes with incompatible schema versions, missing top-level keys, 